.TH COMPARTMENT-ROOT 8 "June 2026" "compartment 1.3.3" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
compartment-root \- full-namespace process isolation (requires root)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B compartment-root
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B compartment-root
creates a fully isolated container using Linux namespaces, then drops
privileges and executes the given command. Requires root. Zero dependencies.
.PP
Isolation is applied in order:
.IP 1. 4
.B clone()
with new UTS, mount, PID, IPC, net, user, and cgroup namespaces.
.IP 2.
.B pivot_root
\- new root filesystem, bind-mounted
.B nosuid,nodev
onto itself first (stronger than chroot).
.IP 3.
.IR /proc " (fresh procfs), " /sys " (read-only sysfs), " /dev
(tmpfs with device nodes bind-mounted from the old root), masked
.IR /proc " paths, isolated hostname."
All of this happens
.I before
the old root is detached \- see
.B HARDENING DETAILS
below.
.IP 4.
.B Capability drop
\- all capabilities removed from bounding set except those named via
.BR \-\-cap\-allowed .
Uses raw prctl + capset (no libcap dependency).
.IP 5.
.B Privilege drop
\- setgid/setuid to service user.
.IP 6.
.B Environment sanitization
and
.B audit logging
(same as
.BR compartment-user (1)).
.IP 7.
.B FD cleanup
\- close all file descriptors >= 3 before the seccomp filter is installed
.RI ( close_range
or fallback loop).
.IP 8.
.BR PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS " (unconditional), " PR_SET_PDEATHSIG ,
then a fork: the command runs as a child of a minimal
.B PID 1
reaper inside the pid namespace.
.IP 9.
.B seccomp BPF
\- raw BPF filter, no libseccomp. A built-in deny-list is installed unless
.BR \-\-block ", " \-\-seccomp\-allowed
or a profile supplies one.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Profile
.TP
.BI \-p ", " \-\-profile " NAME|FILE"
Load policy from a profile file.
Only
.I /etc/compartment/NAME.conf
is searched;
.I $HOME
is never consulted.
An explicit file path (containing \fB/\fR) loads directly.
The file and its containing directory must be owned by root and must not be
group- or world-writable.
Both the
.BI \-\-profile= FILE
and
.BI \-\-profile " FILE"
forms are accepted.
.SS Namespace
.TP
.BI \-c ", " \-\-rootdir " DIR"
Root filesystem directory for pivot_root. Required.
Must be absolute and contain no \fB..\fR components.
.IP
Ownership is checked before
.BR clone (2).
Under the default identity uid map the directory must be owned by
.BR root ;
under a shifted
.B uid\-map
it may instead be owned by the host uid the container's root maps to.
It must never be group- or world-writable: whoever can write the container
root chooses which binaries exist inside it, and the
.B nosuid,nodev
remount only takes the sharpest edge off that.
(A root-owned directory passes the check under a shifted map but cannot
then be used: the container's root is an unprivileged host uid that cannot
create the pivot point.)
.TP
.BI \-u ", " \-\-uid " UID"
Override UID for privilege drop inside the container.
.TP
.BI \-g ", " \-\-gid " GID"
Override GID for privilege drop.
.TP
.BI \-U ", " \-\-username " USER"
Service user for privilege drop. Required.
Looked up via
.BR getpwnam (3)
in the
.B host
.IR /etc/passwd ,
before
.BR clone (2)
\- not in the container's. For a user that exists only inside the
container, give
.BR \-\-uid " and " \-\-gid
explicitly; the name is then used only for diagnostics.
.TP
.BI \-n ", " \-\-netns " NAME"
Join an existing network namespace
.RI ( /var/run/netns/NAME ).
Name must not contain \fB/\fR.
The join happens in the
.I parent
process, before
.BR clone (2),
and
.B CLONE_NEWNET
is dropped so the container inherits the namespace instead of getting a
fresh one.
It has to be done there:
.BR setns (2)
requires
.B CAP_SYS_ADMIN
in the user namespace that
.I owns
the target network namespace, which for anything
.B "ip netns add"
created is the initial one \(em the child, in its own brand-new user
namespace, has that capability nowhere useful.
.IP
One consequence: a network namespace owned by the initial user namespace
cannot have a fresh
.B sysfs
mounted over it from inside the container, so
.I /sys
falls back to an empty read-only tmpfs.
.B \-\-verbose
reports it.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-loopback
Bring up loopback interface in the new network namespace.
.TP
.BI \-C ", " \-\-cgroup " PATH"
Assign the container process to a cgroup. Repeatable.
Path must be absolute, contain no \fB..\fR components, and resolve
(after
.BR realpath (3))
to a location under \fI/sys/fs/cgroup/\fR. The
.I cgroup.procs
file must already exist; it is never created.
.TP
.BI \-M ", " \-\-mount\-mask " PATH"
Hide \fIPATH\fR inside the container: an empty read-only tmpfs over a
directory, a bind of \fI/dev/null\fR over anything else. Repeatable.
Path must be absolute and contain no \fB..\fR components. A path that
does not exist on the running kernel is skipped; a path that exists and
cannot be masked is a fatal error.
.SS "Mount hardening (profile only)"
.TP
.BI rootdir\-flags " LIST"
Comma-separated extra mount flags for the rootdir bind:
.BR ro ", " noexec .
.BR nosuid " and " nodev
are always applied and cannot be turned off, so this directive can only
tighten.
.B ro
is applied
.I last,
after
.IR /proc ", " /dev ", " /sys
and the pivot point have been created in the tree, and deliberately not
recursively \(em those three are separate mounts and stay writable.
.B noexec
on the container root prevents executing
.I anything
inside it, including the target command; it is only useful when the
executables live on a separate mount.
.TP
.BI mount\-ro " PATH"
Bind \fIPATH\fR inside the new root onto itself and remount it read-only.
Repeatable.
The self-bind is necessary because a path inside the container usually has
no mount of its own to carry flags, and the remount is necessary because a
bind mount inherits the flags of its source and
.BR mount (2)
ignores
.B MS_REC
on a remount.
.BR mount_setattr (2)
with
.B AT_RECURSIVE
is used where available (Linux 5.12+) so submounts are covered.
.TP
.BI mount\-noexec " PATH"
As above, with
.BR MS_NOEXEC :
nothing under \fIPATH\fR can be executed. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI mount\-nosuid " PATH"
As above, with
.BR MS_NOSUID :
a setuid binary under \fIPATH\fR does not elevate. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI mount\-nodev " PATH"
As above, with
.BR MS_NODEV :
a device node under \fIPATH\fR cannot be opened. Repeatable.
.SS "Filesystem and network (Landlock)"
.PP
Landlock is
.B off by default
in compartment-root \(em unlike
.BR compartment-user (1),
where it is on. A profile written before Landlock existed here therefore
keeps its previous behaviour. Turn it on with
.B landlock on
in the profile, or with any of the path options below, which imply it.
A policy that carries path rules without turning Landlock on produces a
warning;
.B landlock on
with no path rules is refused, because an empty ruleset with a non-empty
handled mask denies every filesystem access.
.PP
The ruleset is applied after every mount is in place and before the
capability drop, while the child still holds
.B CAP_SYS_ADMIN
in its own user namespace \(em which is what
.BR landlock_restrict_self (2)
needs before
.B no_new_privs
is set. It survives the
.BR setuid (2)
and is inherited by everything the container execs.
.TP
.B \-\-landlock
Enforce Landlock inside the container.
.TP
.BI \-\-ro " PATH"
Read + execute on \fIPATH\fR and everything beneath it. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-rw " PATH"
Read + write, no execute (W^X). Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-rwx " PATH"
Read + write + execute. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-exec " PATH"
Read + execute. On a
.I file
this is a per-binary grant: a policy naming individual files with
.B \-\-exec
and granting execute on no directory is an allow-list of binaries.
The dynamic loader must be listed too \(em
.BR execve (2)
opens the ELF interpreter with
.B FMODE_EXEC
and Landlock checks execute on it \(em while shared libraries need only
read access, because
.B ld.so
opens them read-only and Landlock has no mmap hook.
The grant keys on the file, so a busybox-style multi-call binary cannot be
split into applets.
Repeatable.
.PP
A trailing
.B ?
on any path makes the rule optional; without it, a rule for a path that
does not exist is a fatal error.
Landlock is additive, so a
.B ro
rule inside a
.BR rw / rwx
rule is refused rather than silently doing nothing.
.PP
The profile-only directives
.BR net\-bind " " PORT ,
.BR net\-connect " " PORT
and
.BR net\-default " " deny
add TCP port rules (Landlock ABI v4, Linux 6.7+). They cover
.BR bind (2)
and
.BR connect (2)
on TCP and nothing else: no UDP, no unix sockets, no
.BR listen / accept
granularity and no per-address rules. They are an allow-list; there is no
way to express \(lqeverything except port N\(rq. Below ABI v4
compartment-root warns and the port policy is
.B not
active.
.SS Capabilities
.TP
.BI \-A ", " \-\-cap\-allowed " CAP"
Preserve a capability for the service user. Repeatable.
Accepts \fBCAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE\fR, \fBnet_bind_service\fR, or a
raw number (0\-63). Uses
.BR PR_SET_KEEPCAPS ,
raw
.BR capset (2),
and
.BR PR_CAP_AMBIENT_RAISE .
.SS "Syscalls (seccomp BPF)"
.TP
.BI \-a ", " \-\-seccomp\-allowed " SYSCALL"
Allow-list mode: only permit this syscall. Repeatable.
All other syscalls return EPERM.
.TP
.BI \-B ", " \-\-block " SYSCALL"
Deny-list mode: block this syscall. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-seccomp
Disable seccomp entirely.
.PP
The
.B seccomp\-default
profile directive chooses what a denied syscall does:
.B errno
(the default,
.BR EPERM ),
.B kill
.RB ( SECCOMP_RET_KILL_PROCESS )
or
.B log
(permit and record).
.PP
With none of the above and no \fBblock\fR/\fBallow\fR lines in the
profile, a built-in deny-list is installed (43 syscalls on x86\-64):
ptrace and process_vm_readv/writev; mount, umount2, pivot_root, chroot,
unshare, setns and the new mount API (open_tree, move_mount, fsopen,
fsmount, fsconfig, fspick, mount_setattr); open_by_handle_at and
name_to_handle_at; reboot, kexec_load, kexec_file_load, init_module,
finit_module, delete_module; keyctl, add_key, request_key; bpf,
userfaultfd, perf_event_open, io_uring_setup/enter/register; acct, swapon,
swapoff, settimeofday, clock_settime, clock_adjtime, adjtimex;
pidfd_getfd; ioperm and iopl. Asking for seccomp with an empty filter is
refused rather than run unfiltered.
.SS Environment
.TP
.BI \-E ", " \-\-env\-deny " VAR"
Strip environment variable. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-e ", " \-\-env\-allow " VAR"
Allow-list mode: keep only this variable. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-env\-sanitize
Do not strip environment variables.
.SS General
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be applied without executing.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print actions to stderr. Combined with \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR it also lists
the built-in parts of the policy: the mount plan, every masked path, the
device nodes, the init reaper and the blocked syscalls by name.
.TP
.B \-\-audit
Log events to stderr and to a file.
.TP
.BI \-L ", " \-\-audit\-log " DIR"
Audit log \fBdirectory\fR (implies \fB\-\-audit\fR). The log file
inside it is named \fIYYYY\-MM\-DD.log\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Check system support and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage summary.
.SH HARDENING DETAILS
.SS Mount order and mount_too_revealing
Mounting a fresh
.IR procfs " or " sysfs
from inside a user namespace is gated by the kernel's
.BR mount_too_revealing ()
check: it succeeds only while a fully visible mount of the same filesystem
already exists in the current mount namespace. The container therefore
does
.BR pivot_root (2)
first, mounts
.IR /proc " and " /sys ,
populates
.IR /dev ,
applies the masks, and detaches the old root
.RB ( "umount2(MNT_DETACH)" )
last. Detaching it earlier makes the
.I /proc
mount fail with
.BR EPERM .
.SS Container root
.I rootdir
is bind-mounted onto itself
.RB ( MS_BIND | MS_REC )
and then remounted
.BR nosuid,nodev .
The remount uses
.BR mount_setattr (2)
with
.B AT_RECURSIVE
where available (Linux 5.12+) so submounts are covered too, and falls back
to
.BR MS_REMOUNT | MS_BIND ,
which changes the per-mount flags only \- the superblock and the host's
view of the same filesystem are untouched. A setuid-root binary inside
.I rootdir
therefore does not elevate.
.SS /dev
A
.B tmpfs
is mounted on
.I /dev
.RB ( nosuid,noexec ;
deliberately not
.BR nodev ),
and
.IR null ", " zero ", " full ", " random ", " urandom " and " tty
are bind-mounted into it from the old root.
.BR mknod (2)
cannot be used: the kernel checks
.B CAP_MKNOD
against the initial user namespace, so it always fails here. Each bind is
fatal on failure.
.PP
A private
.B devpts
instance is mounted on
.I /dev/pts
.RB ( newinstance,ptmxmode=0666,mode=0620,gid=5 ,
retried without
.B gid=5
when a shifted gid map leaves the host's tty group unmapped), and that
instance's own multiplexer is bind-mounted over
.IR /dev/ptmx ,
so
.BR posix_openpt (3)
works inside the container.
A 64M
.B tmpfs
.RB ( nosuid,nodev,noexec ", mode 1777)"
is mounted on
.I /dev/shm
for
.BR shm_open (3)
and
.BR sem_open (3).
.IR /dev/fd ", " /dev/stdin ", " /dev/stdout
and
.I /dev/stderr
are symlinked into
.IR /proc/self/fd .
.SS /proc and /sys masking
.I /proc
is a fresh procfs
.RB ( nosuid,noexec,nodev ).
.I /sys
is a fresh read-only sysfs
.RB ( ro,nosuid,noexec,nodev )
with
.I /sys/firmware
masked; if the kernel refuses the sysfs mount,
.I /sys
is covered with an empty read-only tmpfs instead.
.PP
Masked by default inside the container \- an empty read-only tmpfs over a
directory, a bind of
.I /dev/null
over a file:
.IR /proc/acpi ", " /proc/bus ", " /proc/fs ", " /proc/irq ", "
.IR /proc/kallsyms ", " /proc/kcore ", " /proc/keys ", "
.IR /proc/latency_stats ", " /proc/modules ", " /proc/sched_debug ", "
.IR /proc/scsi ", " /proc/sys ", " /proc/sysrq-trigger ", "
.IR /proc/timer_list ", " /proc/timer_stats .
Entries absent on the running kernel are skipped; an entry that exists and
cannot be masked aborts the run. Additional paths can be masked with
.BR \-\-mount\-mask .
.SS User namespace
The parent writes
.B deny
to
.I /proc/<pid>/setgroups
before the gid map (skipped when the policy keeps
.BR CAP_SETGID )
and clears its own supplementary groups before
.BR clone (2).
The map itself defaults to the identity map \fB0 0 65536\fR, which gives
a capability boundary but
.B no uid isolation:
container uid 0 is host uid 0 for DAC purposes. Use the
.BR uid-map " and " gid-map
profile directives to map onto a subuid range instead \-
.I rootdir
must then be owned by the mapped host uid.
.SS Container init
The command does not run as PID 1. PID 1 of the new pid namespace is a
minimal reaper that sets
.B PR_SET_PDEATHSIG
to
.BR SIGKILL ,
forwards
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT ", " SIGHUP " and " SIGQUIT
to the command, reaps orphans, and exits with the command's status
(128+\fIn\fR if it was killed by signal \fIn\fR). The reaper is inside
every namespace and carries the same dropped capabilities, dropped uid and
.B no-new-privs
as the command. It installs the same seccomp filter as the command, but
only when the policy provably permits every syscall its loop uses:
.BR wait4 ", " kill ", " rt_sigaction ", " rt_sigprocmask ", "
.BR rt_sigreturn ", " exit_group " and " write .
.RB ( rt_sigreturn
is on that list because the kernel invokes it to leave a signal handler,
so its absence kills the process the first time a signal is forwarded.)
When the policy does not cover them the reaper stays unfiltered and
.B \-\-verbose
says so: a reaper that dies on its first
.BR waitpid (2)
is a worse failure than one filter fewer on a process that execs nothing.
It execs nothing. Without it a command
with no signal handlers would ignore Ctrl\-C, orphans would never be
reaped, and killing
.B compartment-root
would leave the container running.
.SS Privilege drop sequence
.IP 0. 4
Landlock ruleset applied, when the policy turns it on \- while
.B CAP_SYS_ADMIN
is still held in the container's user namespace.
.IP 1.
Drop bounding-set capabilities (while still root, needs CAP_SETPCAP).
.IP 2.
.B setgid/setuid
to service user.
.IP 3.
If
.B \-\-cap\-allowed
is used: restore named capabilities via capset + ambient raise.
.IP 4.
.B PR_SET_DUMPABLE(0)
\- prevent ptrace from outside.
.IP 5.
Environment sanitization.
.IP 6.
Close all FDs >= 3.
.IP 7.
.BR PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS " and " PR_SET_PDEATHSIG ,
then fork under the PID 1 reaper.
.IP 8.
.B seccomp BPF
filter applied \- to the command always, and to the reaper when the
policy covers what it needs.
.IP 9.
.B execvp
the command.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Basic container:
.B compartment-root \-c /srv/jail \-U svc \-\- /bin/sh
.TP
With capabilities and audit:
.B compartment-root \-c /srv/jail \-U svc \-l \-\-audit \-A net_bind_service \-\- /usr/bin/myapp
.TP
With profile:
.B compartment-root \-\-profile container \-\- /bin/sh
.TP
Preview:
.B compartment-root \-\-profile container \-\-dry\-run \-\- /bin/sh
.SH EXIT STATUS
The container command's exit status is passed through, so any value in
0\-255 is possible.
.TP
.B 0
Command executed successfully inside the container.
.TP
.B 1
Configuration error, namespace creation failure, or privilege drop failure
\- or the command itself exiting 1.
.TP
.BI 128+ n
The command was killed by signal \fIn\fR.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/compartment/*.conf
System-wide profile files.
compartment-root reads no other location.
.TP
.I /var/log/compartment/YYYY\-MM\-DD.log
Default audit log, when
.B \-\-audit
is given without
.BR \-\-audit\-log :
one file per day in a directory created mode 0700.
It must be owned by root and mode 0700; compartment-root refuses to run
if auditing was requested and the directory cannot be used safely.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Linux >= 4.6 (CLONE_NEWCGROUP). Requires root or equivalent capabilities.
No external libraries (no libseccomp, no libcap).
